<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rákóczi's War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1703">1703</a>–<a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rákóczi's War of Independence was the Hungarian uprising of 1703–1711 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, led by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>. Rising from discontent with Habsburg absolutism, taxation and religious repression, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> rebels held much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for several years, but after defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trencsén</a> (1708) the war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szatmar">Peace of Szatmár</a> (1711), which granted an amnesty while Rákóczi went into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rakoczis-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Rákóczi's War of Independence" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: id. Georg Philipp Rugendas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurucok_labancokat_hajtanak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> renounced almost all its claims to Hungarian territories it had held since 1526. Nobles whose lands were returned had to prove their right to them and pay 10 per cent of their worth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, or the property went to the empire's creditors. Peasants suffered from the long wars, and some wished for a return to Ottoman rule.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>, one of the richest landlords in the kingdom, was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> supervision. He was arrested on 18 April 1700 for his correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, escaped from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a> with the help of his wife Amelia and the prison commander, and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where he met Count Miklós Bercsényi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> drew many Austrian troops out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and kuruc forces rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukachevo">Munkács</a> and asked Rákóczi to lead them. On 15 June 1703 about 3,000 armed men under Tamás Esze joined him near Lawoczne, and Bercsényi arrived with French funds and 600 Polish mercenaries. Most nobles held back, calling it a peasant rebellion, but the Hajdús joined him, and by late September 1703 his forces held most of Hungary east and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, then Transdanubia.</p><p>The Diet near Szécsény elected him ruling prince of the Confederated Estates in September 1705. The victory of Austrian and English forces at Blenheim on 13 August 1704 prevented a union with his French-Bavarian allies, and French support dwindled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coinage he issued was poorly accepted, and after 1706 his army retreated.</p><p>At Ónod on 13 June 1707 the Diet declared the deposition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> from the Hungarian throne, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> refused a treaty and no ally came.</p><p>On 3 August 1708 at Trencsén Rákóczi's horse stumbled and he fell unconscious; the kuruc forces thought him dead and fled, a defeat fatal to the uprising. Many leaders then went over to the emperor in hope of clemency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a> mostly fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, while Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusyns">Rusyns</a>, Polish volunteers and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> fought for the kurucs. Rákóczi left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 21 February 1711.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szatmar">Treaty of Szatmár</a>, which granted political and religious amnesty to the kurucs. Though the uprising failed, it prevented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s full integration into the Habsburg Empire, preserved its constitution and kept the powers of its parliament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rákóczi's War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
